> At La Mariposa Montessori, we foster the growth of the whole child by supporting the development of the cognitive, social, emotional and[ spiritual] facets of each unique individual. We nurture the varied capacities of our students by promoting a culture of passionate inquiry, productive engagement, self-regulated time management, and student-led project and event development.
[Our] children are encouraged to set ambitious personal goals, to engage deeply with the topics that are most alluring, to persevere in the face of uncertainty and frustration, and to present their work with high standards of craftsmanship and attention to detail.
[The children]'s[ passion] for learning[ is fueled] also[ by] generous integration of the arts, athletics, and foreign language,[ the] opportunity for direct daily interaction with the animals of[ our] menagerie, and by[ our unique] ability to[ sit]uate academic and cultural studies in the context of the extensive natural env[ir]ons of our campus.
For[ over] a decade,[ our] alumni have[ come back] to thank us for helping them build an incomparable learning platform and inspiring them to make the world a better place[.] Each year they[ return] seeking ways to give back to our community[.
Our] Mont[essor]i classrooms are organized[ into] multi-age cohorts,[ in] which students have[ the] ability to learn from one another, teach one another, and work with the same teacher[ long enough] to promote deep understanding of individual differences. We offer a rich academic curriculum without the stresses of a standardized[ delivery] timetable, enabling children to[ satisfy] their intellectual appetites and initiate work that is unique to them. Because students[ stay] with the same[ teacher for] several[ years], lessons and follow-up work can be responsive to the interests and needs of the children.
Being an independent school, we are not[ bound] by the strict[ chronological] group[ings] and "September only[" transitions that are commonplace] in[ U.]S. schools. The Montessor[i] method informs a true[ responsiveness] to the development of the individual. Teacher, parents and child are able to make crucial educational decisions based on the unique needs of each child.
We call our teachers "guides", because[ our] work is not so much to tell children what we know as to assist their natural[ urge] to become high functioning members of society. Montessori guides are specially trained to understand the particular developmental tasks and[ sensit]ivities of the age group cohorts they work with. New lessons and materials are introduced in ways that promote an[ appetite] for more. Montessori learning environments are prepared to serve the[ development] of deep concentration, self-confidence, initiative and self-regulated social interaction.

